1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,238
done, I would strongly encourage people
not to believe what they read

2
00:00:05,238 --> 00:00:11,811
in the mainstream media about how
well Ukraine is doing in this war

3
00:00:11,811 --> 00:00:18,018
and how poorly Russia is doing,
The West is leading Ukraine

4
00:00:18,018 --> 00:00:23,189
down the primrose path, and the Israelis
are executing a genocide in Gaza.

5
00:00:23,256 --> 00:00:26,926
It's not China's fault
that it wants to be powerful.

6
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:31,231
So if you get into trouble
and you dial 911,

7
00:00:31,231 --> 00:00:34,234
there's nobody at the other end.

8
00:00:34,334 --> 00:00:35,668
Hi, this is John Mearsheimer.

9
00:00:35,668 --> 00:00:38,772
And in today's episode, I'd like to talk

10
00:00:38,805 --> 00:00:44,711
about the situation in Ukraine,
what's happening in the war

11
00:00:44,711 --> 00:00:50,283
between Russia on one side and Ukraine
and the West on the other side.

12
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:54,354
I think if you read the newspapers

13
00:00:54,554 --> 00:00:58,324
in the West every day
and you listen to the mainstream media

14
00:00:58,324 --> 00:01:02,195
in the West, and this is certainly true
in Ukraine as well,

15
00:01:02,429 --> 00:01:06,366
you get a picture
that the Russians are in real trouble.

16
00:01:06,766 --> 00:01:09,169
They're struggling in this war.

17
00:01:09,169 --> 00:01:11,604
The future looks bleak for the Russians.

18
00:01:11,604 --> 00:01:16,676
And in contrast to the Russian situation,
the Ukrainians are actually doing

19
00:01:16,676 --> 00:01:21,648
quite well, especially since they've
adopted drones on the battlefield.

20
00:01:21,648 --> 00:01:25,218
And they're waging this long range
drone campaign.

21
00:01:25,218 --> 00:01:28,755
And one has the sense that

22
00:01:28,955 --> 00:01:31,791
at least we have a stalemate here.

23
00:01:31,791 --> 00:01:35,295
And it may be the case
that the Ukrainians are turning the tide.

24
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:39,666
And when negotiations eventually start,

25
00:01:40,567 --> 00:01:43,636
the Ukrainians will be
in a quite favorable position.

26
00:01:43,636 --> 00:01:46,072
And of course, we're not talking
just about the Ukrainians.

27
00:01:46,072 --> 00:01:47,574
We're talking about the Ukrainians

28
00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:51,611
and the West,
because they are something of a tag team.

29
00:01:52,212 --> 00:01:55,315
I disagree
almost completely with this view.

30
00:01:55,348 --> 00:01:58,351
I've long argued, as many viewers know,

31
00:01:58,618 --> 00:02:02,555
that the Russians would eventually win
and ugly victory.

32
00:02:02,589 --> 00:02:06,092
And I think if you look at
what's actually happening in the war,

33
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:09,095
the Russians are winning the war

34
00:02:09,095 --> 00:02:12,832
and the Ukrainians
are actually in terrible shape.

35
00:02:13,066 --> 00:02:17,370
Again, I don't think you get this picture
from looking at

36
00:02:17,403 --> 00:02:22,075
what's in the mainstream media
or listening to policymakers

37
00:02:22,075 --> 00:02:26,779
in the West speak,
but I think they're wrong.

38
00:02:26,779 --> 00:02:31,818
I think there's a propaganda campaign
at play here, and I want

39
00:02:31,851 --> 00:02:35,855
to tell you a story that is at odds
with this propaganda campaign.

40
00:02:35,889 --> 00:02:39,792
I want to tell you how
I think about the war, and I want to first

41
00:02:39,792 --> 00:02:43,863
focus on the battlefield,
what's going on between

42
00:02:43,863 --> 00:02:47,634
both sides, the Ukrainians
and the Russians on the battlefield

43
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:53,106
that I want to talk about the strategic
bombing campaign or the air war

44
00:02:54,207 --> 00:02:58,244
that is being waged between the two sides.

45
00:02:58,745 --> 00:03:01,648
Then I want to talk about the state

46
00:03:01,648 --> 00:03:04,651
of outside support for Ukraine.

47
00:03:04,951 --> 00:03:08,454
And here we're talking
about Western support for Ukraine,

48
00:03:08,454 --> 00:03:12,458
what it looks like today
and where it might be headed.

49
00:03:12,792 --> 00:03:15,195
Then I want to talk a bit about politics

50
00:03:15,195 --> 00:03:19,098
or the political situation
inside of Ukraine itself.

51
00:03:19,699 --> 00:03:21,167
And then I'll sum up by

52
00:03:21,167 --> 00:03:24,871
talking about where the war is today
and where I think it's headed.

53
00:03:25,004 --> 00:03:29,709
So let me start by first talking
about the battlefield.

54
00:03:29,909 --> 00:03:35,715
And the key to keep in mind here
is that this is a war of attrition.

55
00:03:35,748 --> 00:03:39,419
I don't think this is news to

56
00:03:39,485 --> 00:03:44,224
anybody who's watching this video,
but I do think you want

57
00:03:44,224 --> 00:03:48,228
to keep in mind that this war

58
00:03:49,229 --> 00:03:51,464
is a slugfest.

59
00:03:51,464 --> 00:03:54,500
This is a case of two armies standing

60
00:03:54,534 --> 00:03:57,537
toe to toe on the battlefield.

61
00:03:58,271 --> 00:04:00,540
And in a war of attrition.

62
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:06,946
What really matters more than
anything else is the balance of manpower,

63
00:04:06,946 --> 00:04:11,150
or the balance of troops
that each side has,

64
00:04:11,150 --> 00:04:14,587
and the balance of weaponry,

65
00:04:15,054 --> 00:04:19,592
and in many ways that can be reduced
to the balance of firepower.

66
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:23,863
So let me talk
first about the balance of forces

67
00:04:23,863 --> 00:04:27,500
or the balance of troops
up on the front lines.

68
00:04:28,268 --> 00:04:31,337
I think that the Russians

69
00:04:31,337 --> 00:04:36,309
at this point have a significant
advantage, which is hardly surprising

70
00:04:36,309 --> 00:04:42,348
because the population differences
between the two sides is so great,

71
00:04:42,382 --> 00:04:45,752
and because the troops are drawn
from the population of one side

72
00:04:45,785 --> 00:04:49,622
has a much bigger population,
you would expect that side

73
00:04:50,089 --> 00:04:53,626
to have more troops up on the front lines
than the other side.

74
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:57,196
And I would estimate that at this point,

75
00:04:57,230 --> 00:05:00,433
the Russians have a population advantage

76
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:04,370
of roughly four and a half to one.

77
00:05:04,404 --> 00:05:08,941
It's probably close to 5
to 1 at this point in time,

78
00:05:08,941 --> 00:05:12,378
but that's really a massive advantage
for the Russians.

79
00:05:12,412 --> 00:05:13,379
Just think about that.

80
00:05:13,379 --> 00:05:16,616
Even if it's just 4 to 1, a 4

81
00:05:16,616 --> 00:05:20,620
to 1 advantage in population size

82
00:05:21,654 --> 00:05:24,290
favoring the Russians tells you
they're going to have

83
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:27,927
a lot more troops up on the front line
than the Ukrainians are.

84
00:05:28,227 --> 00:05:31,230
And of course, that is the case.

85
00:05:31,331 --> 00:05:36,169
We know that the Russians
have somewhere on the order of a

86
00:05:36,235 --> 00:05:40,907
2 to 1 to 3 to 1 advantage
in front line troops.

87
00:05:40,907 --> 00:05:44,944
And this problem for the Ukrainians
is exacerbated

88
00:05:44,944 --> 00:05:48,414
by the fact
that they have a significant problem

89
00:05:48,514 --> 00:05:53,286
with draft dodging,
and they have a significant problem

90
00:05:53,286 --> 00:05:58,124
with AWOL, AWOL, or people
who run away from the battlefield.

91
00:05:58,124 --> 00:06:01,127
They're soldiers who were sent up
to the battlefield to fight,

92
00:06:01,127 --> 00:06:04,564
but at some point
they flee the battlefield.

93
00:06:05,498 --> 00:06:09,435
And we know from listening

94
00:06:09,469 --> 00:06:15,408
to the former minister of defense
in Ukraine speak

95
00:06:15,408 --> 00:06:21,547
that Ukraine has a huge problem
with both AWOL and draft dodging.

96
00:06:21,547 --> 00:06:26,085
So here you have an army
that's probably outnumbered,

97
00:06:26,185 --> 00:06:30,890
probably close, close to 3 to 1
in terms of number of troops

98
00:06:31,324 --> 00:06:35,628
that has inside it a significant AWOL
and draft dodging problem.

99
00:06:36,028 --> 00:06:41,467
So the balance of forces favors
the Russians over the Ukrainians.

100
00:06:41,701 --> 00:06:46,472
Moreover, when you talk about the balance
of forces up on the front lines,

101
00:06:46,606 --> 00:06:50,076
you have to take into account the force
to space ratio.

102
00:06:50,076 --> 00:06:51,377
In other words.

103
00:06:51,377 --> 00:06:55,848
Do the Ukrainians have enough troops up
on the front lines

104
00:06:55,848 --> 00:07:01,020
to thickly populate
the forward edge of the battle area,

105
00:07:01,687 --> 00:07:05,658
or do they have a lack of troops,
which produces

106
00:07:05,658 --> 00:07:09,695
a situation
where there are gaps in the front line?

107
00:07:09,729 --> 00:07:14,066
Remember, there's a 1200 kilometer
front line that has to be covered,

108
00:07:14,133 --> 00:07:19,505
and there is an abundance of evidence
coming from Ukrainian sources that the

109
00:07:19,505 --> 00:07:25,311
Ukrainians do not have enough troops
to thickly populate the front lines.

110
00:07:25,711 --> 00:07:28,514
And what's really saved them

111
00:07:28,514 --> 00:07:31,184
in this situation has been drones.

112
00:07:31,184 --> 00:07:33,553
Drones have been something of a stopgap.

113
00:07:33,553 --> 00:07:37,790
In other words,
the Ukrainians can leave holes

114
00:07:37,790 --> 00:07:42,829
in the front line
and then fill those lines with drones.

115
00:07:43,896 --> 00:07:44,230
And then

116
00:07:44,230 --> 00:07:48,100
if the
Russians do push through a particular gap,

117
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:51,604
they can move reserve troops
to close the gap.

118
00:07:51,604 --> 00:07:55,374
But this is a significant problem
for the Russians.

119
00:07:55,408 --> 00:07:59,245
So you see,
when you look at the number of troops up

120
00:07:59,245 --> 00:08:06,018
on the front line, the Russians
significantly outnumber the Ukrainians.

121
00:08:06,018 --> 00:08:10,556
And number two,
the Ukrainians do not have enough troops

122
00:08:10,556 --> 00:08:13,526
to thickly populate the front lines.

123
00:08:13,693 --> 00:08:18,431
Then we go to the matter of weaponry,
and here we're talking about firepower.

124
00:08:18,664 --> 00:08:21,767
There's no question
that throughout the entire war,

125
00:08:23,636 --> 00:08:26,205
the Russians have had a significant

126
00:08:26,205 --> 00:08:30,910
advantage in artillery,
and nothing has changed in that regard.

127
00:08:30,910 --> 00:08:35,314
And if anything, the Ukrainians
are having more difficulty than ever

128
00:08:35,348 --> 00:08:40,286
getting artillery from the West,
and that includes the United States.

129
00:08:40,286 --> 00:08:46,292
And one thing you want to remember about
Russia is that it has a very formidable

130
00:08:46,292 --> 00:08:50,696
industrial base
that can produce huge numbers

131
00:08:50,696 --> 00:08:53,733
of weapons on a monthly basis.

132
00:08:53,733 --> 00:08:58,337
So there's no shortage of artillery
on the Russian side,

133
00:08:58,571 --> 00:09:03,276
whereas there is a shortage of artillery
on the Ukrainian side.

134
00:09:03,276 --> 00:09:07,413
And as I'm sure most of the viewers know,
throughout the course of this war

135
00:09:07,580 --> 00:09:12,785
that has been talk about the Ukrainians
being outnumbered in terms of artillery,

136
00:09:13,352 --> 00:09:15,955
sometimes 5 to 1, sometimes 7 to

137
00:09:15,955 --> 00:09:18,925
1, and even sometimes 10 to 1.

138
00:09:18,925 --> 00:09:22,261
Furthermore,
and this cannot be underestimated,

139
00:09:22,295 --> 00:09:27,900
the Russians
have a huge inventory of glide bombs

140
00:09:28,367 --> 00:09:32,505
that they have been launching
at the Ukrainian

141
00:09:32,505 --> 00:09:36,475
forces on the ground
pretty much since the start of the war.

142
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:40,413
These
glide bombs are basically smart bombs.

143
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:44,250
They're old fashioned iron bombs
that have a special kick

144
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:47,253
put on them that allows

145
00:09:47,653 --> 00:09:51,791
those bombs to land with precision

146
00:09:52,191 --> 00:09:54,827
on the chosen target.

147
00:09:54,827 --> 00:10:00,766
And estimates are that
the Russians have launched about 100,000

148
00:10:00,967 --> 00:10:05,538
glide bombs against Ukrainian forces
since the war started,

149
00:10:05,538 --> 00:10:09,775
and they're now averaging somewhere
in the range of 200

150
00:10:09,809 --> 00:10:14,447
glide bombs
per day against Ukrainian forces.

151
00:10:14,447 --> 00:10:16,215
So when you think about the balance

152
00:10:16,215 --> 00:10:19,986
of firepower here,
you have to take into account or a tillery

153
00:10:20,419 --> 00:10:23,756
and you have to take into account
these glide bombs.

154
00:10:23,756 --> 00:10:27,927
And the Russians,
as I said, have a significant advantage

155
00:10:27,927 --> 00:10:31,464
in terms of artillery
and in terms of glide bombs.

156
00:10:31,464 --> 00:10:38,104
I just pointed out what an impressive
arsenal of glide bombs the Russians have.

157
00:10:38,137 --> 00:10:42,808
At the same time, the Ukrainians
have hardly any glide bombs at all.

158
00:10:42,875 --> 00:10:47,313
So the Russians have an overwhelming
advantage in terms of glide

159
00:10:47,346 --> 00:10:51,550
bombs, a significant advantage
in terms of artillery.

160
00:10:51,684 --> 00:10:56,155
The only way, the only place
where you have something of a balance

161
00:10:56,155 --> 00:11:01,060
in terms of firepower up on
the front lines has to do with drones.

162
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:05,431
And there's no question
that both the Russians and Ukrainians

163
00:11:05,431 --> 00:11:10,069
have a formidable force of drones
upon the front line.

164
00:11:10,069 --> 00:11:14,573
And as I said before,
these drones have played a key role

165
00:11:14,607 --> 00:11:18,744
in allowing the Ukrainians to compensate
for the fact

166
00:11:18,744 --> 00:11:23,949
that they don't have enough troops
to cover the front line.

167
00:11:24,784 --> 00:11:30,389
So drones have been a savior in a
very important way for the Ukrainians.

168
00:11:30,389 --> 00:11:31,257
And I would argue

169
00:11:31,257 --> 00:11:35,061
that in the absence of drones,
this war would have been over

170
00:11:35,061 --> 00:11:39,498
a long time ago, because without drones,
both the balance of firepower

171
00:11:39,498 --> 00:11:44,870
and the balance of manpower is so lopsided
in Russia's favor,

172
00:11:44,870 --> 00:11:48,708
of course, that I think the
Russians would have rolled up

173
00:11:50,209 --> 00:11:52,978
the Ukrainians a year or two ago.

174
00:11:52,978 --> 00:11:55,981
But the fact is that
drones are ever present

175
00:11:55,981 --> 00:11:59,652
on the front lines, and there you have
a rough balance of power.

176
00:11:59,852 --> 00:12:02,755
Now, what about Russian casualties?

177
00:12:02,755 --> 00:12:06,292
You hear all these stories in the news

178
00:12:06,659 --> 00:12:10,396
about the astronomical Russian

179
00:12:10,396 --> 00:12:13,466
casualty levels on the front lines.

180
00:12:13,466 --> 00:12:17,803
And there are these stories
that have appeared

181
00:12:17,970 --> 00:12:22,942
that say
that your average Russian conscript

182
00:12:22,975 --> 00:12:30,449
will only live 20 to 30 hours
when he is sent to the front lines.

183
00:12:30,449 --> 00:12:33,853
Just think about the argument
is that you take a Russian conscript,

184
00:12:33,853 --> 00:12:37,890
you send them to the front lines
and he'll be dead somewhere

185
00:12:37,890 --> 00:12:42,128
between 20 and 30 hours
after arriving at the front lines.

186
00:12:43,062 --> 00:12:46,065
John Ratcliffe,
who is the head of the CIA,

187
00:12:47,233 --> 00:12:50,369
has actually repeated this argument.

188
00:12:50,736 --> 00:12:56,742
In this argument, of course, fuels
the claim that the Russians are suffering,

189
00:12:56,776 --> 00:13:01,580
suffering massive casualties
on a daily basis, and well over

190
00:13:01,580 --> 00:13:05,851
100,000 Russians have died or have been

191
00:13:06,585 --> 00:13:08,854
severely wounded.

192
00:13:08,854 --> 00:13:11,857
These numbers are ridiculous.

193
00:13:12,024 --> 00:13:16,061
There's no question that the Russians have
suffered a few hundred thousand deaths.

194
00:13:16,061 --> 00:13:18,631
I don't deny that for one second.

195
00:13:18,631 --> 00:13:21,167
This is deadly combat, for sure.

196
00:13:21,167 --> 00:13:24,403
But the idea that your average
Russian recruit

197
00:13:24,537 --> 00:13:28,808
recruit only lasts somewhere between 20

198
00:13:28,808 --> 00:13:32,845
to 30 hours is,
I think it's fair to say, ludicrous.

199
00:13:33,813 --> 00:13:36,916
And in fact, if you look carefully

200
00:13:36,916 --> 00:13:40,352
at how the Russians are waging the war,

201
00:13:40,586 --> 00:13:45,991
they are waging it in a way
that is designed to minimize

202
00:13:45,991 --> 00:13:47,493
the number of casualties.

203
00:13:47,493 --> 00:13:50,429
This cannot be emphasized enough.

204
00:13:50,429 --> 00:13:54,433
The Russians are moving very slowly
and very carefully,

205
00:13:54,433 --> 00:13:58,070
and they are privileging firepower

206
00:13:58,337 --> 00:14:01,106
over manpower in their assaults.

207
00:14:01,106 --> 00:14:05,711
They want to do everything they can
to use firepower to eliminate

208
00:14:05,711 --> 00:14:12,618
the Ukrainian forces before
they bring soldiers to bear in the fight.

209
00:14:12,651 --> 00:14:18,858
And the reason here is
that the Russians are deeply interested

210
00:14:18,858 --> 00:14:24,630
in not seeing their fighting forces die,
unless in large numbers.

211
00:14:24,663 --> 00:14:30,369
I mean, you occasionally see reference
to the fact that Russian commanders force

212
00:14:30,402 --> 00:14:33,906
their troops to engage in frontal attacks

213
00:14:33,906 --> 00:14:36,909
where they get mowed down by

214
00:14:37,409 --> 00:14:39,979
Ukrainian firepower.

215
00:14:39,979 --> 00:14:42,381
There's
no evidence that this is happening.

216
00:14:42,381 --> 00:14:45,484
The Russians are not engaging
in frontal attacks.

217
00:14:45,484 --> 00:14:46,619
They're moving forward

218
00:14:46,619 --> 00:14:50,756
in a very methodical way,
depending very heavily on firepower.

219
00:14:50,756 --> 00:14:54,727
As I said before, to clear the way
for the attacking forces

220
00:14:54,793 --> 00:14:59,064
and doing everything they can
to keep the number of casualties.

221
00:14:59,064 --> 00:14:59,765
Damn.

222
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:04,536
On the other hand, it's quite clear
that the Ukrainians

223
00:15:04,536 --> 00:15:08,073
are suffering significant casualties.

224
00:15:09,375 --> 00:15:13,646
Budanov,
who is the head of or was formerly

225
00:15:13,646 --> 00:15:16,815
the head of Ukrainian intelligence,

226
00:15:17,349 --> 00:15:21,153
has recently said that the Ukrainians

227
00:15:21,186 --> 00:15:25,391
need 30,000 new troops every month.

228
00:15:25,858 --> 00:15:29,828
If the Ukrainians need 30,000 new troops
every month.

229
00:15:30,062 --> 00:15:33,065
This implies that they're losing

230
00:15:33,065 --> 00:15:36,035
30,000 troops every month.

231
00:15:36,035 --> 00:15:39,305
Now, some of those troops
may have been lost by.

232
00:15:41,407 --> 00:15:44,576
By desertion, by the troops going AWOL.

233
00:15:44,743 --> 00:15:46,545
That's a serious possibility.

234
00:15:46,545 --> 00:15:49,782
So let's say they're not losing
30,000 troops

235
00:15:49,782 --> 00:15:53,419
in terms of casualties every month.

236
00:15:54,019 --> 00:15:56,855
And when we talk about casualties
here, we're talking about

237
00:15:56,855 --> 00:15:59,091
not we're not talking about people
who are lightly wounded.

238
00:15:59,091 --> 00:15:59,892
We're talking about people

239
00:15:59,892 --> 00:16:03,662
who were killed or severely wounded
and cannot come back into the fight.

240
00:16:03,696 --> 00:16:08,267
So let's assume that the Ukrainians
are losing 25,000.

241
00:16:08,467 --> 00:16:09,501
So we're modifying

242
00:16:10,736 --> 00:16:13,072
number of 30,000.

243
00:16:13,072 --> 00:16:17,810
And if
they're losing 25,000 troops a month,

244
00:16:17,910 --> 00:16:23,749
they have to replace those 25,000,
25,000 over 12 months.

245
00:16:23,782 --> 00:16:25,684
Is 300,000 troops.

246
00:16:25,684 --> 00:16:29,455
That would mean that Ukraine,
according to its own

247
00:16:29,455 --> 00:16:34,460
numbers, is losing 300,000 troops a year.

248
00:16:34,493 --> 00:16:40,899
That is a huge number for an army
that is outnumbered to begin with.

249
00:16:41,066 --> 00:16:45,137
So the point that I'm making to you
here, with regard

250
00:16:45,137 --> 00:16:48,874
to Russian casualties
and Ukrainian casualties,

251
00:16:48,907 --> 00:16:53,512
is that these claims about the numbers
that the Russians are

252
00:16:54,546 --> 00:16:56,982
losing or wildly inflated,

253
00:16:56,982 --> 00:17:00,619
and at the same time,
you do not want to lose sight

254
00:17:00,652 --> 00:17:04,790
of the number of casualties
that the Ukrainians are suffering.

255
00:17:04,790 --> 00:17:08,794
And of course, this is hardly surprising
when you take into account

256
00:17:08,827 --> 00:17:12,831
that the balance of firepower,
when you look at things like artillery

257
00:17:12,831 --> 00:17:18,604
and glide bombs, greatly favors
the Russians over the Ukrainians.

258
00:17:18,871 --> 00:17:22,508
Now, one other issue
we want to talk about regarding

259
00:17:22,508 --> 00:17:26,545
what's happening on the battlefield
is the fact that

260
00:17:26,545 --> 00:17:30,849
the Russians are taking territory
very slowly.

261
00:17:30,983 --> 00:17:34,820
Many people look at the slow pace
at which the Russians

262
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:38,223
are capturing territory, and they conclude

263
00:17:38,257 --> 00:17:41,293
that this is evidence that the Russians

264
00:17:41,293 --> 00:17:45,097
are failing to execute this war

265
00:17:45,264 --> 00:17:48,767
in what one might call a satisfactory way.

266
00:17:49,068 --> 00:17:51,336
I have a couple of points to make here.

267
00:17:51,336 --> 00:17:55,707
First of all, one of the reasons
that the Russians are taking territory

268
00:17:55,707 --> 00:17:59,945
very slowly is because they're moving
very cautiously, and they're moving

269
00:17:59,945 --> 00:18:05,050
very cautiously and very deliberately
because they want to minimize casualties.

270
00:18:05,084 --> 00:18:10,089
My point, my previous point
about minimizing casualties is linked

271
00:18:10,689 --> 00:18:14,626
to this point about the speed at which
the Russians are taking territory.

272
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:17,830
There's no question
that they're taking territory very slowly.

273
00:18:17,830 --> 00:18:21,700
And they're doing that again
because they want to minimize casualties.

274
00:18:22,367 --> 00:18:26,738
Furthermore,
the Russians are fighting on terrain

275
00:18:26,738 --> 00:18:30,509
where they have to go into large

276
00:18:30,542 --> 00:18:33,612
towns and cities and capture

277
00:18:33,612 --> 00:18:37,249
those cities, and to defeat the Ukrainians

278
00:18:37,249 --> 00:18:41,253
in those large towns
and in those cities is a difficult task.

279
00:18:41,253 --> 00:18:43,355
And it's a time consuming task.

280
00:18:43,355 --> 00:18:44,656
And there's no way.

281
00:18:44,656 --> 00:18:47,659
As the Russians move from east to west

282
00:18:48,026 --> 00:18:50,329
in areas like the Donbass,

283
00:18:50,329 --> 00:18:55,534
that they can take towns and cities
quickly, it's just not in the cards.

284
00:18:55,901 --> 00:18:59,938
The Ukrainians are clearly fighting
fiercely in those cities,

285
00:18:59,938 --> 00:19:03,542
and it takes time for the Russians
to capture territory.

286
00:19:03,542 --> 00:19:08,247
But the point we want to keep in mind here
is that the Russians are slowly but

287
00:19:08,247 --> 00:19:14,253
steadily capturing Ukrainian territory,
more and more Ukrainian territory.

288
00:19:14,253 --> 00:19:18,690
And at the same time, and this is
very important in a war of attrition.

289
00:19:19,291 --> 00:19:23,228
The Russians are killing

290
00:19:23,395 --> 00:19:26,732
war, badly
wounding large numbers of Ukrainians,

291
00:19:26,732 --> 00:19:32,905
and making it very difficult
for the Ukrainians to stay in the fight.

292
00:19:33,172 --> 00:19:37,276
So I think when you put all these factors
together and you look at what's happening

293
00:19:37,576 --> 00:19:41,747
on the front lines, there's no question
in my mind that the Russians

294
00:19:41,747 --> 00:19:46,785
are winning on the battlefield,
that the Ukrainians are losing.

295
00:19:46,785 --> 00:19:48,820
And given drones.

296
00:19:48,820 --> 00:19:52,691
It has been difficult for the Russians
to knock out the Ukrainians.

297
00:19:52,691 --> 00:19:54,626
There's no question about that.

298
00:19:54,626 --> 00:19:58,764
But at the same time,
there is no question that the Ukrainians,

299
00:19:58,764 --> 00:20:01,967
given the fact that they're outnumbered
in terms of troops

300
00:20:02,334 --> 00:20:05,404
and that they're outnumbered
in terms of firepower,

301
00:20:06,171 --> 00:20:11,343
that they are destined
to lose on the battlefield.

302
00:20:11,376 --> 00:20:16,248
Now, let me switch gears
and talk about the air campaign.

303
00:20:17,049 --> 00:20:19,618
And let me first
just talk about the inventory

304
00:20:19,618 --> 00:20:22,621
of weaponry on both sides,

305
00:20:22,888 --> 00:20:28,493
and then talk about the strategies
that both sides are employing

306
00:20:28,493 --> 00:20:33,932
and how those strategies are likely
to affect the outcome of the war.

307
00:20:34,700 --> 00:20:38,470
First of all,
let me talk about the Russian inventory.

308
00:20:38,537 --> 00:20:44,476
The Russians have a huge inventory
of ballistic missiles and cruise missiles

309
00:20:44,476 --> 00:20:48,780
that they have been using
against the Ukrainians.

310
00:20:49,381 --> 00:20:54,219
Most estimates are
that the Russians have launched about 6000

311
00:20:54,886 --> 00:20:59,725
ballistic missile and cruise
missile attacks against Ukraine,

312
00:20:59,725 --> 00:21:05,364
in addition to the ballistic missiles
and the cruise missiles the Russians have.

313
00:21:05,430 --> 00:21:10,869
As I said before, these glide bombs,
these smart bombs that they can launch

314
00:21:10,869 --> 00:21:16,575
from the air and with pinpoint accuracy,
hit targets on the ground.

315
00:21:16,575 --> 00:21:20,712
And as I said before, they've launched
100,000 glide bombs.

316
00:21:20,746 --> 00:21:24,650
In addition,
the Russians are now getting assistance

317
00:21:24,650 --> 00:21:28,687
from North Korean short
range ballistic missiles

318
00:21:28,687 --> 00:21:32,224
because the North Koreans
are fighting side by side

319
00:21:32,224 --> 00:21:37,629
with the Russians, and they are bringing
ballistic missiles with them.

320
00:21:37,796 --> 00:21:40,632
And very importantly,

321
00:21:40,632 --> 00:21:43,635
the Russians have a sophisticated

322
00:21:44,169 --> 00:21:48,674
inventory of drones, jet powered drones.

323
00:21:48,707 --> 00:21:52,444
The Geron four and the drone five are jet

324
00:21:52,511 --> 00:21:57,115
powered drones
that are giving the Ukrainians fits.

325
00:21:57,549 --> 00:22:02,454
If you look at the Ukrainian media,
if you look at the Kyiv Independent,

326
00:22:02,454 --> 00:22:05,924
for example, it's
quite clear that the Russians

327
00:22:05,924 --> 00:22:09,795
are having a powerful effect

328
00:22:10,295 --> 00:22:14,533
in terms
of waging the air war against Ukraine

329
00:22:14,533 --> 00:22:18,403
with all of these new jet powered drones

330
00:22:18,503 --> 00:22:24,376
that the Ukrainians are having
a very difficult time shooting down.

331
00:22:24,376 --> 00:22:28,213
And these drones
are not only difficult to shoot down,

332
00:22:28,780 --> 00:22:33,618
but they are also very accurate
because they are steered to the target

333
00:22:33,618 --> 00:22:37,389
by ground operators,
by Russian ground operators.

334
00:22:37,789 --> 00:22:39,825
They're not just simply preprogramed

335
00:22:39,825 --> 00:22:43,462
to go after a particular target
and then sent on their way.

336
00:22:43,862 --> 00:22:46,832
On the contrary,
they're basically maneuvered to

337
00:22:46,832 --> 00:22:52,904
their target by ground based operators,
and they fly at reasonably high altitudes.

338
00:22:52,904 --> 00:22:57,576
They have the ability to maneuver, and
Ukrainians are having a devil of a time

339
00:22:57,576 --> 00:23:00,579
dealing with this massive inventory

340
00:23:00,579 --> 00:23:03,915
of jet powered drones
that the Russians have.

341
00:23:03,949 --> 00:23:07,753
So when you look at
what the Russians have, you see again,

342
00:23:07,786 --> 00:23:10,822
a huge inventory of ballistic
missiles, cruise

343
00:23:10,822 --> 00:23:15,227
missiles,
glide bombs and jet powered drones.

344
00:23:15,227 --> 00:23:20,632
And now they have North Korean ballistic
missiles at their disposal as well.

345
00:23:21,066 --> 00:23:24,636
If you look at what the Ukrainians have,

346
00:23:24,669 --> 00:23:28,407
as I said before,
they have virtually no glide bombs.

347
00:23:29,408 --> 00:23:30,776
They have

348
00:23:30,776 --> 00:23:34,613
virtually no ballistic missiles
of any consequence.

349
00:23:34,646 --> 00:23:38,483
They do have some cruise missiles,
mainly cruise missiles

350
00:23:38,483 --> 00:23:40,352
that they've gotten from the West.

351
00:23:40,352 --> 00:23:45,824
These are the storm shadows
that they've gotten from the British,

352
00:23:47,125 --> 00:23:52,264
but they have not gotten large numbers
of cruise missiles.

353
00:23:52,264 --> 00:23:55,267
The number of cruise missiles that the

354
00:23:55,834 --> 00:24:01,807
Ukrainians are firing at the Russians
pales in comparison to the number

355
00:24:01,807 --> 00:24:06,211
of cruise missiles that the Russians
are firing at the Ukrainians.

356
00:24:07,045 --> 00:24:09,214
And there's no reason

357
00:24:09,214 --> 00:24:12,350
to think that's going to change
anytime soon.

358
00:24:13,418 --> 00:24:17,622
And in terms of drones,
there's no question

359
00:24:17,656 --> 00:24:22,527
that the Ukrainians
have a significant drone force.

360
00:24:23,094 --> 00:24:25,897
This is a situation
that's analogous to the battlefield.

361
00:24:25,897 --> 00:24:28,166
To go back to the battlefield.
Remember what I said?

362
00:24:28,166 --> 00:24:33,138
I thought the drones had been Ukraine's
savior on the battlefield.

363
00:24:33,138 --> 00:24:38,577
My argument was that without drones,
Ukraine would have lost

364
00:24:38,610 --> 00:24:42,714
on the battlefield, in my opinion,
at least a year ago, maybe two years ago.

365
00:24:42,747 --> 00:24:45,450
Drones have kept them in the fight.

366
00:24:45,450 --> 00:24:51,389
And in terms of the inventory of strategic

367
00:24:52,190 --> 00:24:56,828
air assets, I think drones are where the

368
00:24:58,296 --> 00:24:59,898
where the Ukrainians have

369
00:24:59,898 --> 00:25:03,835
been, by and large, able
to match the Russians.

370
00:25:04,069 --> 00:25:08,406
They don't have a sophisticated force
of jet powered drones.

371
00:25:08,406 --> 00:25:10,375
That's the equivalent of what
the Russians have.

372
00:25:10,375 --> 00:25:14,112
But nevertheless, there's no question
that the Ukrainians have

373
00:25:14,579 --> 00:25:17,582
a powerful force of drones.

374
00:25:17,949 --> 00:25:20,051
That they have been using.

375
00:25:20,051 --> 00:25:22,687
But otherwise,
if you move away from drones,

376
00:25:22,687 --> 00:25:24,489
you look at all these other weapons.

377
00:25:24,489 --> 00:25:27,025
The Russians have

378
00:25:27,025 --> 00:25:30,028
a decisive advantage
in the number of cruise missiles,

379
00:25:30,028 --> 00:25:34,633
ballistic missiles,
glide bombs and so forth and so on.

380
00:25:35,333 --> 00:25:37,769
So that's the basic inventory.

381
00:25:37,769 --> 00:25:41,406
But what's more important
than the inventory, or at least

382
00:25:41,406 --> 00:25:46,645
as important than the inventory,
is the question of sort of how you employ

383
00:25:47,145 --> 00:25:52,417
those air assets
to affect the outcome of the war.

384
00:25:52,651 --> 00:25:55,554
And I think you can talk about an air

385
00:25:55,554 --> 00:25:58,623
campaign in two different ways.

386
00:25:59,291 --> 00:26:01,726
First, you can envision

387
00:26:01,726 --> 00:26:05,630
an air campaign as a punishment campaign.

388
00:26:06,031 --> 00:26:09,167
And with a punishment campaign,

389
00:26:09,301 --> 00:26:12,304
you end up killing lots of people

390
00:26:12,537 --> 00:26:18,176
or wrecking the other side's economy
or damaging the other side's

391
00:26:18,176 --> 00:26:22,047
economies in ways that cause great

392
00:26:22,047 --> 00:26:26,251
pain for the population and force.

393
00:26:26,651 --> 00:26:31,823
You force the population to put pressure
on the government to end the war.

394
00:26:32,791 --> 00:26:35,427
So the idea is that if you

395
00:26:35,427 --> 00:26:42,300
hit a city
and you take out the energy network

396
00:26:42,300 --> 00:26:48,673
in that city and people don't have water
or they don't have heating,

397
00:26:48,707 --> 00:26:52,744
people will rise up and put pressure
on the government to end the war.

398
00:26:52,744 --> 00:26:55,914
Or the government itself will say,
enough is enough.

399
00:26:56,581 --> 00:26:59,551
So much
pain is being inflicted on our population.

400
00:26:59,551 --> 00:27:00,452
So much punishment

401
00:27:00,452 --> 00:27:05,056
is being inflicted on our population
that this war has to be ended.

402
00:27:05,090 --> 00:27:09,394
That's one way
you can think about employing air power.

403
00:27:09,494 --> 00:27:15,100
Second way you can think about employing
air power is you tear apart

404
00:27:15,166 --> 00:27:18,870
military infrastructure in the rear

405
00:27:18,870 --> 00:27:22,073
areas of your adversary,

406
00:27:22,073 --> 00:27:28,046
and you wreck the other side's
economy in ways

407
00:27:28,046 --> 00:27:33,785
that affect its ability
to wage war up on the front lines.

408
00:27:34,252 --> 00:27:38,089
So you're not in this second
with this second strategy.

409
00:27:38,156 --> 00:27:41,393
You're not punishing
the civilian population.

410
00:27:41,426 --> 00:27:45,563
You're not causing the civilian population
to rise up against the government

411
00:27:45,563 --> 00:27:49,100
or causing the government to say
enough is enough

412
00:27:49,100 --> 00:27:52,604
because the civilian population
is being so badly hurt.

413
00:27:52,671 --> 00:27:56,141
No, the second strategy calls

414
00:27:56,141 --> 00:28:02,247
for doing great damage to the economy
and to military infrastructure

415
00:28:02,247 --> 00:28:07,752
in ways that influences
the fighting between the two sides.

416
00:28:08,253 --> 00:28:11,256
Now, I think up until recently,

417
00:28:12,057 --> 00:28:15,927
the Russian campaign
really focused on the first strategy.

418
00:28:15,927 --> 00:28:19,764
It was really,
by and large, a punishment campaign.

419
00:28:21,833 --> 00:28:25,970
And I think
if you look at what Ukraine is doing,

420
00:28:25,970 --> 00:28:31,276
Ukraine strategy has been
and still is a punishment campaign.

421
00:28:31,276 --> 00:28:35,213
And when we talk about Ukraine's air
campaign here, we're talking about it's

422
00:28:35,580 --> 00:28:37,615
it's drone campaign, right.

423
00:28:37,615 --> 00:28:39,451
Basically a punishment strategy.

424
00:28:39,451 --> 00:28:42,554
And as I say, I think the Russians
have been waging a punishment

425
00:28:42,554 --> 00:28:45,724
strategy up until recently as well.

426
00:28:45,890 --> 00:28:48,893
Punishment strategies just don't work.

427
00:28:49,294 --> 00:28:53,832
Just look at the damage
that Russia has done

428
00:28:54,099 --> 00:28:59,003
with its air war against Ukraine
over the past few years, and there is zero

429
00:28:59,003 --> 00:29:03,708
evidence that it has caused Ukraine
to think about quitting the war.

430
00:29:05,076 --> 00:29:06,945
And the same thing is true when you look

431
00:29:06,945 --> 00:29:10,248
at Ukraine's drone campaign,

432
00:29:10,315 --> 00:29:14,686
this is its long range drone campaign
against the Russian heartland.

433
00:29:14,753 --> 00:29:18,223
There's no way that's
going to cause Russia to quit the war.

434
00:29:18,256 --> 00:29:22,260
Punishment campaigns don't work,
and the Ukrainian punishment

435
00:29:22,260 --> 00:29:26,064
campaign against
Russia is a much less potent

436
00:29:26,698 --> 00:29:30,268
punishment campaign
than the Russian campaign against Ukraine.

437
00:29:30,268 --> 00:29:34,939
And again, the Russian punishment campaign
did not get Ukraine to quit.

438
00:29:35,473 --> 00:29:37,008
But what's happened here?

439
00:29:37,008 --> 00:29:40,779
What's changed in the Russian campaign,

440
00:29:41,246 --> 00:29:46,484
the Russian strategic bombing campaign,
is that it has gone from a punishment

441
00:29:46,484 --> 00:29:51,356
campaign to a campaign
that's designed to wreck

442
00:29:51,990 --> 00:29:55,393
the Ukrainian economy in ways

443
00:29:55,393 --> 00:30:00,932
that will significantly influence
events on the battlefield.

444
00:30:00,932 --> 00:30:04,469
And that bombing campaign is also

445
00:30:04,769 --> 00:30:08,306
now directed at influencing

446
00:30:08,773 --> 00:30:11,543
actual military events

447
00:30:11,543 --> 00:30:17,916
by going after targets
in the rear areas of Ukraine

448
00:30:17,916 --> 00:30:22,253
that contain military infrastructure.

449
00:30:22,287 --> 00:30:26,191
Now, what exactly am I talking about here
in recent months?

450
00:30:26,424 --> 00:30:31,196
What Russia has done it is

451
00:30:31,229 --> 00:30:36,534
that is turned Ukraine
into a landlocked country.

452
00:30:37,168 --> 00:30:40,939
The importance of this
cannot be underestimated

453
00:30:41,072 --> 00:30:44,142
because Ukraine depends

454
00:30:44,142 --> 00:30:47,612
heavily on its Black Sea ports

455
00:30:48,012 --> 00:30:51,349
to export and import

456
00:30:51,549 --> 00:30:55,954
agricultural goods, industrial goods

457
00:30:55,954 --> 00:31:01,693
and military
wherewithal, military wherewithal.

458
00:31:01,693 --> 00:31:03,094
You want to think about that?

459
00:31:03,094 --> 00:31:08,933
So what's happened here is that
Russia has concentrated on shutting down

460
00:31:08,967 --> 00:31:13,905
ports like Odesa
and other ports along the Black Sea.

461
00:31:13,938 --> 00:31:17,675
These are Ukrainian ports,
which is which has made it

462
00:31:17,675 --> 00:31:23,181
virtually impossible
for Ukraine to import and export

463
00:31:23,348 --> 00:31:28,286
goods, be
they military or economic, at those ports.

464
00:31:28,286 --> 00:31:33,291
And this is why I said that
what's happened here is Russia has turned

465
00:31:33,324 --> 00:31:36,327
Ukraine into a landlocked country.

466
00:31:36,594 --> 00:31:41,366
This is having devastating effects
on the Ukrainian economy.

467
00:31:41,366 --> 00:31:44,135
There's no way that
that could not be the case.

468
00:31:44,135 --> 00:31:47,138
And we know from back in 2022,

469
00:31:47,739 --> 00:31:53,044
when those ports were shut down
for a number of months that it had

470
00:31:53,111 --> 00:31:58,016
then it then had devastating effects
on the Ukrainian economy.

471
00:31:58,016 --> 00:32:00,885
So there's no question
that given that the Russians

472
00:32:00,885 --> 00:32:04,822
have shut down
those Black Sea ports, turned Ukraine

473
00:32:04,822 --> 00:32:09,661
into a landlocked country,
that this is going to go to great lengths.

474
00:32:09,661 --> 00:32:15,500
This will go to great lengths to damage
severely the Ukrainian economy.

475
00:32:15,500 --> 00:32:19,170
And this matters
because the Ukrainian economy

476
00:32:19,337 --> 00:32:23,541
healthy or a reasonably healthy
Ukrainian economy or functioning

477
00:32:23,675 --> 00:32:27,178
Ukrainian economy, is essential
for supporting

478
00:32:27,178 --> 00:32:30,181
the troops on the front line.

479
00:32:30,214 --> 00:32:35,353
On top of the damage that's being done
to the Ukrainian economy,

480
00:32:35,353 --> 00:32:38,690
the fact is that lots of Russian,

481
00:32:39,390 --> 00:32:44,729
lots of excuse, excuse me,
lots of Ukrainian military material

